WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.777 --> 00:00:07.860
ACE NASSRI: My name's Ace.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.290
I'm in Developer Programs
Engineer here at Google Cloud,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.640
and today we're going
to be talking about how

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.900
to keep up with serverless.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.283
So design pattern evolutions
for the scalable future.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.700
So first I wanted
to take a moment

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.117
to clear up some
confusion on what

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:21.642
we mean when we say serverless.

00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:23.600
A lot of people think
when you hear serverless,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.090
oh that's compute,
that things like

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:28.260
AWS Lambda, or Azure Functions,
or Google Cloud Functions.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.850
But here at Google, we define
serverless as a little bit more

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:31.940
broadly than that.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.300
So in particular, when we're
talking about serverless

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.830
we're referring to
any platform that

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:39.320
has the following traits,
saying not just compute.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.780
So the first of these traits
is plug-and-play auto-scaling,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.280
which essentially means that
no matter how many requests you

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.000
throw at this thing
it'll scale up

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.950
and scale down within reason.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.865
The second key trait
of serverless platforms

00:00:50.865 --> 00:00:52.240
is that they
support what's known

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.190
as scale-to-zero behavior.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.920
So this means that
essentially if you

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.830
don't direct any requests
at your serverless platform

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.030
that it will not charge
you for that period.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.458
So not only do they scale up
and scale down based on load,

00:01:05.458 --> 00:01:07.000
they can also scale
down to the point

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.042
where they won't charge
you if you don't actually

00:01:09.042 --> 00:01:10.810
direct any you load at them.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.780
So this combined with the
typical low to no configuration

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.360
experience of many of
our serverless platforms

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.878
means that they have a uniquely
low initial cost to get

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.670
started on, especially
if you're developing

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.600
a totally new application.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.240
Now, last but not least
serverless platforms

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.100
are provider managed.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.620
And what this means is back
in the old days if you wanted,

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.500
say, a NoSQL database,
you had to worry

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.720
about the infrastructure
underlying that database as

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.310
well as the database itself.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.650
Nowadays we can
say, OK, hey Google,

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:39.692
give me a NoSQL database.

00:01:39.692 --> 00:01:42.108
I don't want to worry about
the underlying infrastructure,

00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:43.690
I want you to take care of that.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.480
And Google can say, yes,
that's something we can do,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.920
that's called Cloud
Firestore, Cloud Datastore.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.360
So a couple other
things I wanted

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.040
to cover in terms of high
level caveats with serverless--

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.623
and this is all
background by the way,

00:01:56.623 --> 00:01:58.790
we'll get to the good
stuff in a couple slides.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.340
So the first high
level caveat here

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.830
is that the way serverless
does scalability

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.630
is sort of no questions asked.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.130
I'll get into more
detail about this

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.789
later, but suffice to say
that this can backfire.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.610
Second, the
additional abstraction

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.010
inherent in the
serverless model means

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.420
that it's typically
easier to use

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.970
and easier to get started
with, but it's also

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.670
less visible and
configurable when

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.170
it comes to your deployments
versus, say, the infrastructure

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.500
as a service model.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.352
So quick plug for
serverless hosting on GCP.

00:02:29.352 --> 00:02:30.810
I just want to take
the opportunity

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.352
to level set everyone
in the audience

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:33.690
as to what our products are.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.030
So off to the left we have
our functions offering,

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.850
Google Cloud Functions,
and those essentially

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.820
take tiny snippets of code
and scale those to whatever

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.700
the demand may be.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.380
to the right of that we
have App Engine, which takes

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.410
applications-- effectively
sets of URL routes

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.528
that map to functions.

00:02:50.528 --> 00:02:53.070
To the right of that, we have
our newest surveillance product

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.500
called Cloud Run,
which will allow

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.250
you to run containers in a
serverless and scalable manner.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.500
Now, those three are sort
of true serverless options,

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.590
if you will, because
they support all three

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:05.695
of those traits.

00:03:05.695 --> 00:03:08.070
To the right of that we have
some serverless-like options

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.660
such as Cloud Run on GKE--

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.320
Google Kubernetes Engine-- or
perhaps even raw GKE itself,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.027
and those are not
quite serverless

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:16.860
because they don't have
all of those traits.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.068
In particular they require
some degree of management,

00:03:19.068 --> 00:03:20.682
they are not provider managed.

00:03:20.682 --> 00:03:22.140
But they have some
of those traits,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.170
so they're sort of
serverless-like in nature.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.840
Now, when you're
trying to decide

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.260
which one of these products
is right for you, of course,

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.050
there could be reams
written about the subject.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.850
But generally what you want to
do is start off to the left,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.670
start off with the
most abstract option,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.440
and then work your
way right as you

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.240
find that the abstract
options don't fit

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.040
your needs for whatever reason.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.890
So enough about the intro.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.453
I wanted to take some time now
to go over some common patterns

00:03:50.453 --> 00:03:51.870
that we'll see
within our patterns

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.040
ourselves, sort of
meta-patterns, if you will.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.650
So the first common
trait that we're

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.270
going to see in
these patterns today

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.580
is we're going to see lots
of asynchronous behavior,

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.130
we're going to see lots of
things like message queues.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.442
Second, we're going to
see lots of decomposition.

00:04:05.442 --> 00:04:07.150
So we're going to take
these large tasks,

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.010
decompose them into
many smaller ones

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.050
that can then,
third, be distributed

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.710
amongst a series
of many machines.

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:18.010
So fourth, we can have
non-serverless components

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:19.683
in our serverless architectures.

00:04:19.683 --> 00:04:21.100
There are a lot
of caveats to this

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.650
which we'll touch on in a later
slide, but suffice to say,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:26.452
yes, it can be done.

00:04:26.452 --> 00:04:28.160
And last but not least,
I kind of alluded

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.620
to this in the previous
slide, self-hosted primitives

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.380
like databases are increasingly
becoming provider managed.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.070
And the so-what of that
is you, the developer,

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.050
is less concerned
about the ops and more

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.390
concerned about whatever
the platform does.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.490
So you're not
thinking of this as,

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.950
oh, this is a NoSQL database
running on a Linux instance,

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.460
you just-- this is
a NoSQL database.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:47.960
That's it.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.970
So in terms of the
benefits of these patterns,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.220
the first big benefit is
that distributed architecture

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.200
gets a lot easier compared
to the pre-serverless world.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.600
So when it comes to things like
Kubernetes and orchestration,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.390
they're increasingly
becoming sort of optional.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.980
If you want that
fine grained control

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.870
you can get it through
things like Knative.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.453
But if you're like me, you just
want to deploy an application,

00:05:12.453 --> 00:05:14.910
have it scale, then you can
rely on things like serverless

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.150
to kind of abstract that
ops, that orchestration away

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.970
for you.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.460
And of course, distributed
architecture primitives

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.400
are no different.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.400
They are also transitioning
to serverless just

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.620
like the general cloud
primitives, as well.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.410
And last but not
least, these serverless

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.560
distributed architectures
are more or less just as

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.940
scalable as the old school
distributed architectures.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.850
So we don't really lose that
much in terms of scalability

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.028
by moving to serverless.

00:05:40.028 --> 00:05:41.820
And another thing I
wanted to touch on here

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.820
is that separation of
concerns gets a little easier.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.920
Or rather, there are
things we can do now

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.780
with serverless to increase
our separation of concerns.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.733
So oftentimes separation
of concerns is helpful.

00:05:52.733 --> 00:05:54.150
It will make your
code base easier

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.150
to work with, and to go
through, and to maintain.

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.962
Not always, sometimes
it can backfire.

00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:01.170
This is one of those things
where it might make sense

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:02.760
for you, it might not.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.560
Judge based on your
particular fact situation.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.902
And of course, serverless
and these other new patterns

00:06:07.902 --> 00:06:09.360
like the microservice
pattern allow

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.510
us to achieve higher degrees of
separation in our code bases.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.290
And now that we've talked about
some of the motivating factors

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.180
behind serverless,
some of why we do it,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.400
I wanted to take a moment
to touch on the things

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.420
that we want to change going
into the serverless world.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.860
So of course there are some
things we want to change,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.890
but there are also
things we want to keep.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.640
And because we're all
positive people here

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.840
we're going to focus on the
things we want to keep first.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.390
So the first thing
we want to keep

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.730
is the presence of this idea
of separation of concerns.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.510
So in particular, we probably--

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.480
if you're coming from the old
application development world,

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.982
you might have heard
of this M/V/C idea.

00:06:49.982 --> 00:06:51.440
And essentially
what that is is you

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.940
take a client
facing application,

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:54.900
you break it down
into three components,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.650
you have your models, your
views, and your controllers.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.200
Now for those of you who are
not familiar with this model--

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.298
no pun intended--

00:07:01.298 --> 00:07:03.590
basically what happens is
your models are your database

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:05.420
schema, your views
are your front end,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.128
and then your controllers
are essentially

00:07:07.128 --> 00:07:07.962
your business logic.

00:07:07.962 --> 00:07:10.170
Now of course this was a
step in the right direction,

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.337
but for reasons we'll get
into later we can actually

00:07:12.337 --> 00:07:13.530
do better than this pattern.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:14.780
Another thing that
we want to keep

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.380
is how we do distributed
architecture.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.410
So we knew back then
that it could give us

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.390
less coupling and higher
throughput compared

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:21.930
to non-distributed
architectures,

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.930
and we also knew that it
was one of the few ways

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.120
to truly scale our
application simply

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.490
because vertical scaling
can only work up to a point

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.740
more or less given
technological restrictions,

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.630
and after that our
only other option is

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:35.270
horizontal scaling which is
by definition a distributed

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:36.983
system.

00:07:36.983 --> 00:07:38.650
The last thing I
wanted to touch on here

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.350
is how we do things
in the front end.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.780
You might be familiar with
this idea of thick clients.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.762
Basically you take all
your business logic

00:07:45.762 --> 00:07:47.470
and instead of putting
it on the back end

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:50.560
you kind of move most of
it towards the front end.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.060
Now, it turns out
that serverless

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.090
allows us to create uniquely
lightweight back ends which

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.460
can be a natural fit for
things like thick clients

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:00.780
and single page apps.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.820
So as I said, serverless
back ends naturally

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.425
fit these existing
front end techniques.

00:08:06.425 --> 00:08:07.800
So in terms of
the things that we

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.390
do want to change, the
things that we might want

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.520
to do differently when
we move to serverless,

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.800
the first is the
client-facing app

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.390
patterns and app
design patterns have

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.710
improved a little bit since.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.302
Like I said, it turns out
we can use other patterns

00:08:19.302 --> 00:08:21.510
to achieve a greater degree
of separation of concerns

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.540
than we can with the model
view controller approach.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.130
Also, distributed
systems back then

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.170
were notably harder
to implement.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:31.320
Principally, you had to worry
about ops and/or orchestration.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.570
You probably had to worry
about things like Kubernetes

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:37.190
or unmanaged components within
your distributed system, right?

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:39.840
Now, also another thing
was back then there

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.750
were no real tools for doing
lightweight event processing--

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.450
or excuse me, lightweight yet
flexible event processing.

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.450
The best you could do was
something like if this

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:47.970
then that.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.990
Which is good as long as its
WYSIWYG approach contains

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:53.530
whatever you're
trying to get done,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.488
but the second that it
doesn't you more or less

00:08:55.488 --> 00:08:58.980
can't just write raw code on
top of something like that.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.170
So adding your own custom
logic, like I said,

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:05.310
meant writing your entire
application from scratch.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.055
So that begs the question, well,
what does serverless change?

00:09:08.055 --> 00:09:09.680
What does serverless
bring to the table

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:13.523
that is inherently different
than this pre-serverless world?

00:09:13.523 --> 00:09:14.940
So the first thing
to bear in mind

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.970
is that serverless is more
capital E evolutionary

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.730
than capital R revolutionary.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.350
So the serverless
way naturally is

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.200
more of an evolution of old
approaches than a completely

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.960
new approach in and of itself.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.150
Distributed systems,
the way those work

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.640
is you're starting
to see increasingly

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.470
serverless versions of core
distributed systems primitives.

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.860
So things like caches, message
queues, so on and so forth.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.330
And you're starting
to see the high level

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:45.150
architectural principles
actually not change.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.175
They more or less
stay the same when

00:09:47.175 --> 00:09:49.050
you move from this
non-serverless distributed

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.480
architecture to the
serverless variant.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.960
And as a hands-on demo of
what this looks like, so let's

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.750
say we have a pretty canonical
distributed system here.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.200
It just kind of accepts a
stream of image resize requests,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.210
uses a message queue
to load balance

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.790
those across some
set of compute units,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.220
and those compute units will
then resize those images

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.585
and do something with them,
whether it's returning them

00:10:10.585 --> 00:10:12.960
back or storing them in some
sort of storage or something

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.070
like that.

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.510
Now, this is the old
school way of doing things.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:18.090
We had our message
queue presumably

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.740
running on some sort of
infrastructure as a service.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:23.710
So this might be like
RabbitMQ or whatever.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.370
And then that was load
balancing amongst a bunch

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:28.170
of compute units,
which in this case

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.720
are raw Compute Engine VMs.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.650
Now, the downside of this
is we have to still worry

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:35.820
about that ops component.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.190
Yes it works, yes it's
scalable, but we still

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:42.100
have to worry about the
GCE or GKE ops workload.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:46.320
So if we move to the serverless
version of this, of course

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.392
we have less of
that ops concern.

00:10:48.392 --> 00:10:50.850
But the beauty of this is that
we can kind of transliterate

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.460
these non-serverless components
over here pretty much

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:56.070
just to serverless
components off the bat.

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:57.630
We're not even
saying, OK, well we

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.540
have to do anything different
with our message queue,

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.040
or we have to do
anything different,

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.620
use some sort of different
version of compute.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.440
All we're doing is we're taking
a non-serverless component,

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.380
swapping it out for a
serverless component.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.110
And once we make that change,
making any required code

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.540
changes to mesh
the two together.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.570
And for reference, the products
on the right here, essentially

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.187
what we're doing is we're
taking, again that message

00:11:21.187 --> 00:11:23.520
queue that is running on
infrastructure, swapping it out

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.620
with its serverless alternative
which is something like Google

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:26.550
Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.407
We're taking the
serverful compute

00:11:28.407 --> 00:11:30.240
and swapping it out
with serverless compute,

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:33.810
which in this case
is Cloud Functions.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:37.272
So in terms of what else is
new, so web front end ideas--

00:11:37.272 --> 00:11:38.730
I kind of already
touched on this--

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:41.670
also already fit this
service world quite well.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.550
So notably, things like HTML
templating and static file

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.140
hosting haven't really changed.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:49.170
CDNs, whatnot, they're
all still relevant.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.690
But thick clients is really
where serverless kind of

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:53.190
comes into its own.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:54.470
The idea of a thick client--

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.250
a thick front end is not new.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.920
But once you pair that with
the thin back ends afforded

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:00.840
by serverless,
this means you can

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.480
get really good meshing
between your front end

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.580
and your back end.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:09.820
So as an example of how
this works in the real world

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.550
or with a contrived problem,
admittedly, so let's

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:14.855
say we have this
example pet store here.

00:12:14.855 --> 00:12:16.480
So there are a couple
different actions

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:17.480
that the users can take.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.060
Those are all our views on the
front end, the list, buy, cart

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:23.020
and pay URLs, and then
we're backing this

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.320
with a monolith running on
GKE for its back end compute,

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.690
and then we have a bunch
of primitives or components

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:29.990
of to the right here.

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.800
So you'll see there
that the first component

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:36.310
in that list of primitives
is a SQL database.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:37.990
Now, if you're
thinking, OK, well I

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.540
want to scale this thing,
that SQL database can scale up

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.150
to a point but I'm not going to
be able to horizontally scale

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:43.570
that.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:45.237
I'm eventually going
to hit a bottleneck

00:12:45.237 --> 00:12:47.590
and I'm going to have to get
rid of that SQL database.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:51.070
You might think, OK, well I can
just migrate that SQL database

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.320
to a NoSQL alternative
and boom, I'll

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:55.660
have horizontal scalability.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.010
And that is correct.

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:59.260
This application as it
is, if you architect it

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.020
in the right way, is
horizontally scalable.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:04.270
It will scale up and
scale down to meet demand.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:05.908
But it's not
entirely serverless.

00:13:05.908 --> 00:13:08.200
You still have to worry about
things like orchestration

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:11.980
and ops because you have
your serverful compute,

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.990
you have your
serverful primitives.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:18.340
But if you want to abstract
that ops workload away,

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.830
if you want to start moving
towards serverless, what you

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:22.480
can do is say, OK,
well, I'm going

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.040
to start with the primitives.

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.390
And again, like we did
in the previous example,

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.360
we can transliterate
those primitives-- not

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.850
even translate, transliterate
those primitives--

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:36.045
from their serverful options
to their serverless options.

00:13:36.045 --> 00:13:37.670
So we might look at
this and say, well,

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.380
we have a NoSQL database
and we have a log service.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.130
And those are serverful,
those are running

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:43.670
on some sort of infrastructure.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:46.162
Now, what is a
serverless log service?

00:13:46.162 --> 00:13:47.620
Something like
Stackdriver Logging.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:49.660
What is a service
NoSQL database?

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:52.210
Something like Cloud
Datastore, Cloud Firestore.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.090
So it turns out if we just
do that transliteration

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.372
and then update our code
accordingly we can say,

00:13:57.372 --> 00:13:59.080
OK boom, we've got
our primitives running

00:13:59.080 --> 00:13:59.663
on serverless.

00:13:59.663 --> 00:14:02.170
We've more or less
reduced our ops workload

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.000
by migrating everything.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.910
But there's still the elephant
in the room, or rather

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:09.080
the elephant in the
architecture here,

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.170
which is that monolith
in the middle.

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:13.060
So if you wanted
to do a simple--

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.980
well, not so simple--
if you wanted

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:16.930
to do the easiest
possible lift and shift

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:19.310
to get onto serverless,
you might say, well,

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.700
maybe I want to put that on
some sort of raw serverless

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:25.570
compute option like Cloud
Run or maybe App Engine.

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:27.040
Cloud Run would
probably be ideal

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:29.800
if you wanted the least effort
lift and shift for something

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:31.660
like a GKE cluster.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:34.123
But you're in the
situation where you're

00:14:34.123 --> 00:14:35.290
dealing with a thick client.

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.290
You say, OK, well, maybe
we should take the time

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.270
to re-architect this so we can
really take advantage of not

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:42.160
only serverless
but the advantages

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:46.120
afforded to us by this thick
client on the front end.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:47.890
So what you can do is
say, well, if we're

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:49.677
going to go the extra
mile, let's go ahead

00:14:49.677 --> 00:14:51.760
and migrate this to something
like Cloud Function,

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.190
some sort of serverless
compute option.

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:57.130
And by doing that not only do we
get the benefits of serverless

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.470
in terms of ops and
scalability, but we

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:00.970
build an architecture
that is better

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:04.000
suited to this idea of
the thick client front end

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.160
as opposed to having
this massive back end

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:08.980
and this massive front
end where there's also

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.500
all sorts of logic
duplication, all sorts

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.600
of additional maintenance,
all sorts of bad things

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:17.830
that you're doing on both
for really no good reason.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:21.160
So in terms of what
else is new, what's

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.050
changed a little bit
in terms of abstraction

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:25.630
is back then we only
really had one option,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:27.400
and that was infrastructure.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.620
So that meant that
we had more work,

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:32.590
but we also had more
control and visibility

00:15:32.590 --> 00:15:34.630
over our deployments.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:36.400
Nowadays, the
beauty of serverless

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.010
is that it strictly gives
us additional options.

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:40.870
So in terms of
serverless options,

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:42.880
they give us a lower
getting started

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.740
point, a lower minimum in terms
of the work that we have to do.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:47.620
But if we want that
additional control

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:51.010
and additional visibility we
can still say, hey, serverless

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:53.228
isn't right for me,
I want these things

00:15:53.228 --> 00:15:55.270
so I'm going to go off
towards the infrastructure

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:57.820
side of things.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.760
And as I mentioned earlier
with the default cost model,

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:02.590
it has changed a little bit.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:04.413
For non-serverless
platforms, it used

00:16:04.413 --> 00:16:05.830
to be we would set
a billing limit

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:08.290
and then we would
fail at that point.

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:10.810
So once we hit that
quota, we basically

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:13.870
serve the user a quota exceeded
error or something like that.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:17.050
And the beauty of that is if we
got a sudden deluge of requests

00:16:17.050 --> 00:16:19.900
that we didn't want to fulfill--
say it was a denial of service

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:21.520
attack or a bug in our code--

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.273
then this is the
right approach and we

00:16:23.273 --> 00:16:24.940
wouldn't be charged
for filling requests

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:27.010
that we didn't fulfill.

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.840
Nowadays, the approach
with serverless

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:32.020
is essentially scale
now, pay later.

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:33.850
Now, this approach
works well when

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:36.970
you get the sudden deluge
of legitimate requests.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.810
Say, for example, your marketing
team knocks it out of the park

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.390
and you come into work
next day, and you pick up

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:45.940
your copy of your favorite
national newspaper,

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:49.600
and your app is right
there on the front page.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.880
And suddenly everyone in
the country or everyone

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:55.210
across the world is
trying your application,

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:59.140
and your user base has
gone up by like 10, or 50x,

00:16:59.140 --> 00:16:59.980
or whatever.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.600
In that sort of
situation, you want

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:03.700
to make sure that your
application can scale up

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.579
to handle that demand
rather than serving all

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:08.720
of your new users
a bunch of quota

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.829
exceeded errors, because that
leaves a very bad impression

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:13.369
as to your application.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.310
So the one thing
I do want to note

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:19.250
is that these are defaults,
they can be changed.

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:21.202
So if you are in
a situation where

00:17:21.202 --> 00:17:22.660
you're using
non-serverless but you

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:25.420
want it to have that
unrestricted scaling

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:27.280
capability, you can do that.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:29.283
If you're also in
a situation where

00:17:29.283 --> 00:17:30.700
you want to use
serverless but you

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:32.428
want to restrict its
ability to scale,

00:17:32.428 --> 00:17:33.970
that is something
you can do as well.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.050
So now I'm going to touch on the
good stuff, the actual design

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:41.592
patterns that we're
going to be using

00:17:41.592 --> 00:17:43.210
in the serverless environment.

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.620
So first of all, a quick
intro into the things

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.010
that have changed.

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.820
So the first design pattern that
we do a little bit differently

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.680
is how we do busy wait.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:54.570
So back in the old
days, we used to be

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.190
able to rely on
things like callbacks

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:57.900
or blocking calls
and kind of wait

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.930
around for long running
tasks to complete.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:04.230
Nowadays, we try to rely more
on message passing and things

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.430
like callbacks with their
own separate endpoints,

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.500
which I'll show
in the next slide.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:12.173
So in terms of what this looks
like with a hands-on example,

00:18:12.173 --> 00:18:13.590
the old school way
of doing things

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:15.173
might be to say, OK,
well, we're going

00:18:15.173 --> 00:18:17.430
to do some effectively
instantaneous operation

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.110
like getting a user ID.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.390
Then we're going to
kick off some slow, long

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.665
running external operation,
and then we're going to wait.

00:18:24.665 --> 00:18:26.040
We're going to
walk around stage,

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:30.070
take a sip of our coffee,
twiddle our thumbs,

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:31.720
whatever, go to the bathroom.

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:34.083
And then once that
operation is done,

00:18:34.083 --> 00:18:36.250
now bear in mind while we're
waiting this operation,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.417
we still have that Cloud
Functions instance running.

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:42.040
That is still reserved, that is
still costing us compute time.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:46.030
Now that that operation is
done, then we write the result

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:48.080
to the database.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:51.230
Now, there are two big
problems with this.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:53.780
One, the first thing
is Cloud Functions

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:56.850
has a nine minute
timeout at most.

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:58.730
So if this slow
external operation

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:00.390
takes longer than
nine minutes, what

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:03.170
will actually end up happening
is something like that.

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.030
That last line of
code will not execute,

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:06.770
your function will
throw a timeout error,

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:09.870
and it will cease execution.

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:13.040
Now, the second problem is that
even if this operation does

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.680
complete, even if it, say,
take six or seven minutes,

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.967
you're still paying for seven
minutes worth of compute

00:19:17.967 --> 00:19:19.550
where you're not
really doing anything

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:20.510
during those seven minutes.

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:22.400
You're just kind of
sitting around waiting

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:24.210
for this operation to complete.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.840
So of course that was then and
this begs the question, well,

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:29.660
how can we do this
in a better way?

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:32.300
So it turns out we can
do it in a better way.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:34.613
So what we want to do
is essentially take

00:19:34.613 --> 00:19:37.030
all of our instant operations,
our synchronous operations,

00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:38.602
and put them in one function.

00:19:38.602 --> 00:19:40.810
And then we want to take
that slow external operation

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:43.960
and turn it into some sort
of asynchronous alternative.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.300
Now, there are a couple
ways we can do this.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.940
Some operations such as those
with the Data Loss Prevention

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:51.790
API give us the ability
to pass in a Pub/Sub

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:53.770
topic that will then
be notified when

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:55.570
that operation is complete.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:58.810
So we essentially say, hey,
slow external operation,

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:01.150
when you're done can
you publish a ping

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.160
to this topic-- my topic?

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.860
And that slow external operation
says, yeah sure, I can do that,

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.680
and I can also include this ID
as metadata if you want me to.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:12.542
So once we do that we
can say, OK, great,

00:20:12.542 --> 00:20:13.750
I'm going to go to sleep now.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:14.830
This Cloud Function
is going to go

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.440
to sleep now after kicking
off this external operation,

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:20.110
and it doesn't matter how long
that external operation takes.

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:22.540
Once it's done, that Pub/Sub
message will be fired

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:25.150
and it will trigger the second
Cloud Function that writes

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:27.630
this result to the database.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:29.910
And again, in this
case this solves

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.350
both of those two problems.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:34.200
It solves the timeout
problem because once

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:36.300
that slow external
operation is kicked off

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.460
this Cloud Function can
exit and say I don't

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:39.750
have to do anything anymore.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.820
If it takes all of
100 milliseconds

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.860
to kick that operation
off, then you're

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.833
being charged for 100
milliseconds plus whatever

00:20:46.833 --> 00:20:48.000
it costs to get the user ID.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.958
You're not being charged
for the amount of time

00:20:49.958 --> 00:20:51.710
that the operation is running.

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:53.390
And second, even
if that operation

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:57.140
takes an hour you don't have
to worry about that timeout

00:20:57.140 --> 00:21:00.440
because this Cloud Function
is only running for however

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.880
long it needs to get that ID
and kick off the operation,

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:04.880
not actually wait for the
operation to complete.

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:11.890
So the next pattern
I want to touch on

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:14.470
is how we do session affinity.

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:16.390
And basically the idea
with this is back then

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:18.790
we could rely on session
affinity, which essentially

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:22.900
means that if a user
sends, say, 10 requests,

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:25.573
that those 10 requests will
all go to the same machine.

00:21:25.573 --> 00:21:27.490
You might be using a
distributed architecture,

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.590
you might have
thousands of machines

00:21:29.590 --> 00:21:31.990
across your entire
application, but each user

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.080
will always go to the
same machine more or less.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:39.280
Nowadays with serverless, that's
increasingly not available.

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:42.850
So a user's request may
hit different machines,

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:46.900
and this affects how
we're going to store state

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.840
through those user requests.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.590
The second thing I
wanted to touch on here

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:53.997
is explicitly machine state.

00:21:53.997 --> 00:21:56.080
Not just user state, but
the state of the machines

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:57.170
themselves.

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:59.620
So back then we could
also rely on things

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:00.845
like persistent machines.

00:22:00.845 --> 00:22:03.220
We could rely on saying, OK,
I want to save this to disk,

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:04.840
or I want to say
this to RAM, and I

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:06.700
can trust that when
the user comes back

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:08.900
that it'll still be there.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:10.780
Nowadays, we're
increasingly moving

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:13.310
to ephemeral architectures.

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:17.437
So there are no guarantees
in terms of where

00:22:17.437 --> 00:22:18.520
the user is going to land.

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.770
They might make 10
different requests--

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:23.820
or excuse me, they might
make 10 different requests,

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:26.320
and the machine could start up,
and shut down, and start up,

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:28.510
and shut down 10
different times.

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:30.520
Even if you have
session affinity,

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:32.830
even if it goes through the
same machine every time,

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:35.390
it doesn't really matter
because if that machine was

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:37.390
provisioned, and
deprovisioned, and provisioned,

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:40.450
and deprovisioned, then those
deprovisionings can wipe state

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:41.290
from that machine.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.020
And of course, going
back to session

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:48.070
affinity a little bit,
that is doubly a problem

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:51.100
because even if you have
those persistent machines

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:53.050
it doesn't really
matter if the user could

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:56.140
hit 10 different machines
across 10 different requests.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:58.180
So the solution to
both of these problems

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:00.220
is you're going to want
to persist your data

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:02.590
outside of your actual compute.

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:04.870
So once that request is
done, maybe you send it off

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.730
to some sort of cache
or you send it off

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:10.010
to some sort of Cloud Datastore,
or something like that.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:12.010
The idea is you're not
relying on any state

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:14.230
within the serverless
compute option itself

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:18.720
because that state is
essentially ephemeral.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.500
So a couple more things
I wanted to touch on

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.340
in terms of design patterns.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.330
So first, how we do retries
has changed a little bit.

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:29.570
So retriable operations, it
used to be we kind of had

00:23:29.570 --> 00:23:32.370
to have our own doctor node,
if you will, that would say,

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:33.853
OK, this operation failed.

00:23:33.853 --> 00:23:35.270
I'm going to go
retry that and I'm

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:38.520
going to keep trying this
operation until it succeeds.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.070
Increasingly, that
is being offloaded

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.250
to serverless alternatives such
as serverless message queues.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:47.010
So we can use things like
Pub/Sub and Cloud Tasks

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:50.350
as our sort of artificial doctor
nodes where they will say,

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:53.010
OK, I'm going to retry this
operation-- for example,

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:55.830
for Pub/Sub, I'm going to
try this operation until you

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:58.380
actually acknowledge
receiving my message.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:00.490
And if your operation
fails you simply say,

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:02.880
OK, I'm not going to acknowledge
that Pub/Sub message,

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.650
and it will
automatically send me

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:08.315
another copy of that
message when it's ready.

00:24:08.315 --> 00:24:09.690
Now, another thing
that changes--

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.300
I kind of alluded to this in
some of the previous slides--

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.040
but it's how we
can take advantage

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:15.888
of separation of concerns.

00:24:15.888 --> 00:24:17.430
So used to be back
then we would rely

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:18.972
on something like
the M/V/C approach,

00:24:18.972 --> 00:24:20.683
the model view controller model.

00:24:20.683 --> 00:24:22.350
Nowadays we can take
advantage of what's

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.420
known as the
microservice pattern,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:27.450
and this is a little
bit of a buzzword which

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:29.190
we'll explain the next slide.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.470
But essentially what it
means is that we can achieve,

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:33.512
like I said, that greater
separation of concerns.

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:41.180
So in terms of what this
microservice term actually

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:44.930
means, what we're talking about
here is the idea that apps

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:47.780
are collections
of small services.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:50.420
So each service has its
own narrowly scoped purpose

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:52.710
as well as its own code base.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:55.280
Now, how you store these
code bases is highly

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:57.720
dependent on your
particular situation,

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:00.690
but some common patterns that
we've seen you might say,

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:02.300
OK, well I want to
put all of my code

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:05.138
for my application in one
giant version control repo

00:25:05.138 --> 00:25:06.680
and then have a
bunch of directories,

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.328
each directory corresponding
to a microservice.

00:25:09.328 --> 00:25:11.120
You might also say in
your situation, maybe

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:13.980
it's easier to set up
CI/CD in this manner--

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:17.010
continuous integration
deployment-- you might say,

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:20.420
OK, I want to put each of
these services in its own repo.

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:22.910
Whatever situation
makes sense for you

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:24.420
that's what you should do.

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:27.330
There is no one
right answer here.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:29.900
Now, some of the benefits
of this microservice pattern

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:31.850
versus the M/V/C approach.

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:33.650
First of all, we get
per-service control

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:35.600
of a bunch of different
things, namely

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:37.610
things like scaling,
permissions, and access

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:38.580
control.

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:41.970
We also get the enforcement
of good API design.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.270
Now, this can be a
double-edged sword

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:45.578
depending on your situation.

00:25:45.578 --> 00:25:47.120
If you're working
on something that's

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:48.950
just a quick hack,
a quick prototype,

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.860
you probably don't want
something like this.

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:52.610
You may not want the
microservice pattern.

00:25:52.610 --> 00:25:54.770
But if you're working on a
team with dozens or hundreds

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:56.895
of people and you're trying
to build something that

00:25:56.895 --> 00:26:00.063
is going to last for years
then having that consistent API

00:26:00.063 --> 00:26:02.480
enforcement, that consistent
software engineering practice

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:04.970
can actually be really helpful
in terms of the longevity

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:08.310
of those larger code bases.

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:12.570
And last but not least, this
is one of the key benefits

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:13.710
of microservices.

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:17.430
They give us this language
and stack independence.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:21.270
So for example, if you wanted
to write an application

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:22.740
and there were
five microservices,

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.898
you could write it with
five different stacks,

00:26:25.898 --> 00:26:26.940
five different languages.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.982
You could say, I want to
write these two in Java,

00:26:28.982 --> 00:26:31.770
I want to write this one in C++,
I want to write that one in C#,

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:33.850
and I want to write
that one in Node.

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:36.810
And as long as they have
a common communication

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:40.290
protocol such as JSON over
HTTP, or protobuf, or something

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:42.240
like that, then you're good.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:44.070
as long as they can
talk to each other

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.170
and they know each
other's APIs then

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.180
you can do essentially
whatever you

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:51.540
want in terms of tech stack.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:54.610
So as an example
of how this works,

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.700
here's our canonical
pet store again.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.740
So this is the M/V/C approach.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:01.560
We have our views on the left,
which again, are front ends.

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:02.880
We have our controllers
in the middle, which

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.297
is our business
logic, and then we

00:27:04.297 --> 00:27:05.797
have our models on the right.

00:27:05.797 --> 00:27:07.380
Now if you look at
this, you'll notice

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:09.963
that there's a lot of complexity
going on between the checkout

00:27:09.963 --> 00:27:12.180
method and all these
various models.

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:14.940
In fact, it's connecting to the
user method, the pay method,

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:16.080
and the user cart--

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:19.710
the user model, excuse me-- the
pay method model, and the user

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:21.040
cart model, as well.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.240
So of course, that complexity
means it's very hard for us

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:26.460
to separate those two
components, which kind of makes

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:27.870
us sad.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:30.060
We can't exactly just
go say, oh, I just

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.680
want to reuse
checkout over here,

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:36.370
we have to kind of yank
all those models out of it.

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:40.200
So in terms of what this looks
like in the microservice app

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:43.390
world, you might even
glance at this and say, wow,

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:44.070
this is crazy.

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:47.880
How is this any simpler
than that M/V/C approach?

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:49.530
There's so much
stuff going on here.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:53.170
But the reality is if you
look at it in the right way,

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:55.770
you'll start to see, OK, well,
the separation of concerns

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:57.265
looks something like this.

00:27:57.265 --> 00:27:59.640
So you have your pet service,
which has its own database,

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:01.890
has its own back end
logic, and all it

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.230
cares about are
three APIs methods,

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:06.690
list pets, add pet,
and remove pet.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:09.930
So if it's its own create,
read, update, delete information

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:11.700
storage service,
the beauty of this

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:13.830
is you can change
whatever internal details

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:17.100
of the pet service that you
want to without having to worry

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:19.020
about the rest of the services.

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:21.810
The only time you need to
worry about the other services

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:24.960
is when you're changing
the external facing APIs.

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:27.750
Now of course, because it's
the microservice architecture,

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:30.630
the cart service, same thing.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.310
You could say, OK,
it's running on Java,

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.110
I want to code it in Python.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:36.960
It's running on Python, I want
to move it to C#, whatever.

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:39.570
As long as that API
interface remains the same,

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:42.192
you can change essentially
whatever you want.

00:28:42.192 --> 00:28:44.150
And of course, the pay
service is no different.

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:45.360
It's also isolated.

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:47.010
Now, note that
this does call out

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:49.290
to those other microservices.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.650
So you would have to update it
if those microservices changed,

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:55.530
but again, the actual
inner back end logic here

00:28:55.530 --> 00:29:00.933
is totally isolated from
what happens elsewhere.

00:29:00.933 --> 00:29:03.350
So in terms of bridging the
gap between these two worlds--

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:06.170
between the pre-serverless world
and the serverless world we

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:07.345
live in today--

00:29:07.345 --> 00:29:09.470
the first thing you're
going to want to be aware of

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:12.440
is that among many
different problems

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:14.780
there's one really
big one, and that

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:18.620
is this idea of non-scalable
or not quite serverless parts.

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:21.110
If you have parts that are
scalable but not serverless,

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:22.820
they're a little
easier to deal with.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.430
But non-scalable parts mean
that you have to do things

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:27.050
a little bit differently.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.000
So notably what you're
going to want to do

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:32.480
is treat this as essentially
what academics would call

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:34.250
a producer-consumer problem.

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:35.750
Which means you
have a producer, you

00:29:35.750 --> 00:29:37.640
have something producing
tasks, and then you

00:29:37.640 --> 00:29:40.040
have a consumer that is
consuming those tasks.

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:41.720
Now, your producer
might be serverless,

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:43.250
your producer might be scalable.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:44.822
But if your consumer
is not, you want

00:29:44.822 --> 00:29:47.030
to make sure you don't
overload your consumer with so

00:29:47.030 --> 00:29:49.220
many requests that it
buckles under the pressure.

00:29:49.220 --> 00:29:52.280
So what you can do is use
Pub/Sub, or Cloud Tasks,

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:56.930
or some sort of serverless
message queue as a task buffer.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:30:01.430
And that buffer will throttle
how many tasks are coming out

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:03.950
within a given amount of
time or within a given

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:07.640
set of parameters and ensure
that your downstream service

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:09.830
that can only handle,
say, x tasks a minute

00:30:09.830 --> 00:30:15.830
or x tasks per whatever period,
or x tasks at once does not get

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:17.540
overloaded with so
many tasks that it

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:20.070
buckles under the pressure.

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:23.390
And if you need additional
fine tuned logic on top of this

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:26.270
you can use things like App
Engine or Cloud Functions

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:28.910
to give you that
additional consumer logic.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:31.700
Now, this fine tuned logic might
be that, say, Pub/Sub and Cloud

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:34.993
Tasks don't support exactly the
throttling policies you want

00:30:34.993 --> 00:30:36.410
so then you have
to implement them

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:38.340
in some sort of serverless
compute option--

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:39.715
which because it's
serverless can

00:30:39.715 --> 00:30:41.600
handle that volume of requests.

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:44.060
Or it might be that you
want to take those requests,

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:46.100
you want to perform some
modification to them,

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:47.720
and only then do you
want to send them

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:49.250
to your downstream service.

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:51.590
Regardless of which one of
those situations you're in,

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:53.673
having this additional
layer of serverless compute

00:30:53.673 --> 00:30:56.040
can help you achieve that.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.100
So some rules of
thumb when it comes

00:30:58.100 --> 00:30:59.420
to migrating to serverless.

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:03.620
Note of course, this is a
very, very, very complex topic.

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.120
If you or your
company are seriously

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:09.080
considering undertaking this
journey, what I recommend

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:11.060
is you check out the
talk by my colleagues

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:13.850
Martin Omander and Adam
Ross, Serverless 202

00:31:13.850 --> 00:31:15.560
from Monolith Microservices.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:18.230
They will take you through
this in a lot more detail

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:19.767
than I am about to now.

00:31:19.767 --> 00:31:22.100
But essentially what you want
to remember, first of all,

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:24.013
serverless is not magic.

00:31:24.013 --> 00:31:26.180
Things like data dependencies,
things like slow I/O,

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:28.750
things like application
security, these still exist.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:29.750
They are still concerns.

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:33.000
Serverless is helpful,
but it's not a panacea,

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.180
it does not solve everything.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.550
So the second thing, when
you're doing this migration

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:39.110
you generally
speaking want to do it

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:40.790
slowly and piece by piece.

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:42.830
Because if things
break, it's easier

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:44.750
to isolate what the
cause of that breakage

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:50.960
was if you go slow and
don't rush it through.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.930
Now, you also don't want
to go all or nothing

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:56.270
in terms of old approaches.

00:31:56.270 --> 00:31:58.730
Like I said, serverless is
more capital E evolutionary

00:31:58.730 --> 00:32:00.477
than capital R revolutionary.

00:32:00.477 --> 00:32:02.060
So you want to take
advantage of this,

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:04.580
you want to take advantage
of the sort of prior art

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:07.430
that exists in this old
world that will enable you

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:10.190
to leverage that to build your
new serverless architecture.

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:13.400
You don't want to, say,
reinvent the wheel, so to speak.

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.410
And last but not
least, when it comes

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:18.170
to making your app totally
serverless this will give you

00:32:18.170 --> 00:32:20.223
a slight boost
because at that point,

00:32:20.223 --> 00:32:22.640
you won't have to worry about
things like ops anymore more

00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:23.540
or less.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.550
You'll have a much
more scalable app

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:28.190
if you're dealing with
non-scalable components.

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:29.780
But the thing to
bear in mind is this

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:32.510
is not a reason to migrate
your application all at once.

00:32:32.510 --> 00:32:34.310
The benefit from going
totally serverless

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:37.790
is totally outweighed
by the costs that

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:40.910
may be imposed by migrating
your app all at once if anything

00:32:40.910 --> 00:32:41.720
goes wrong.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:45.660
So definitely despite this, this
is a reason to see it through,

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:50.350
but it's not a reason to rush
that migration process, though.

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:53.050
So now that we've touched
on some of the things that

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:56.650
have changed, some of the 0 to 1
changes in terms of serverless,

00:32:56.650 --> 00:33:00.490
I wanted to take a moment
to talk about the capital R

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:03.220
revolutionary aspect
of serverless.

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:04.600
So these are new
patterns that we

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:08.200
can take advantage of that
were not really available to us

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:09.970
before services and
things like functions

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:11.690
as a service showed up.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:13.150
So the first of
these patterns is

00:33:13.150 --> 00:33:16.010
how we do event
processing and automation.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:19.300
So it used to be that if we
wanted lightweight deployments,

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:21.580
we had to use some
sort of-- well,

00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:23.218
to be honest they didn't exist.

00:33:23.218 --> 00:33:25.010
The best we could do
is use infrastructure,

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:26.740
which was kind of heavyweight.

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:28.630
Even if we said, OK,
here's this 10 lines

00:33:28.630 --> 00:33:32.080
of code, every time I get a
Gmail message, run this code.

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:35.320
That would mean setting up
a whole entire application

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:38.350
for those 10 lines of
code, and it just really

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:39.850
wasn't worth it in
terms of the time

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:42.820
we'd save by writing
all this automation.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:45.460
Nowadays, what we can do is
increasingly rely on just

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.140
individual functions-- single
functions-- and we say,

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:50.710
hey, all I have to do if I want
to run this 10 lines of code

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:52.585
is write the 10 lines
of code and stick it up

00:33:52.585 --> 00:33:53.740
on Cloud Functions.

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:57.170
I don't have to worry about, OK,
I wrote the 10 lines of code,

00:33:57.170 --> 00:33:59.020
now I have to set up,
say, infrastructure,

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:02.120
I have to set up maybe a LAMP
stack, or something like that.

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:06.430
Another thing I want to touch on
here is the ubiquity of events.

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:10.185
So things like webhooks and
Pub/Sub are common today,

00:34:10.185 --> 00:34:11.560
but they're becoming
increasingly

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:14.260
common in the future.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:17.110
Now, webhooks are common
with external platforms.

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.510
What you'll also
see with Pub/Sub

00:34:19.510 --> 00:34:22.360
is that you can
access a lot of events

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:24.520
through things like
Stackdriver Logging.

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:28.210
So as we mentioned previously
in that busy wait slide

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:31.132
not all operations will
have access to a Pub/Sub

00:34:31.132 --> 00:34:32.590
notification when
they're complete,

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:35.489
but a lot of
operations will instead

00:34:35.489 --> 00:34:37.900
log the Stackdriver when
that operation is finished.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:39.675
So that's something
where you can say,

00:34:39.675 --> 00:34:42.050
OK, well I'm going to take
that Stackdriver notification,

00:34:42.050 --> 00:34:43.883
I'm going to export
that Stackdriver Logging

00:34:43.883 --> 00:34:45.340
notification to
Pub/Sub, and then

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:48.190
Pub/Sub is going to use that
to trigger a Cloud Function.

00:34:48.190 --> 00:34:51.699
So essentially-- and there's a
tutorial on this on our site.

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:54.707
If you go to
cloud.google.com/function/docs,

00:34:54.707 --> 00:34:57.040
there's a tutorial that walks
you through how to trigger

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:00.170
a Cloud Function in response
to a Stackdriver notification.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:05.530
So the TLDR here is not only are
webhooks and Pub/Sub becoming

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:09.070
increasingly common, but various
other services like Stackdriver

00:35:09.070 --> 00:35:11.325
are also starting
to feed into Pub/Sub

00:35:11.325 --> 00:35:12.700
that can essentially
be converted

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:15.180
into Pub/Sub notifications.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:17.770
And of course, G
Suite is no different.

00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:20.370
So G Suite integration is also
becoming easier through things

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:22.620
like REST APIs, and
this type of thing

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:26.970
allows you to take advantage
of line of business automation

00:35:26.970 --> 00:35:29.610
and making your day to day
workflow more efficient

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:32.340
using various forms of code.

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:34.950
For those of you that have seen
any of the automation charts

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.590
out there, there's a
particularly famous one

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:39.840
from a certain web comic.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:43.417
But essentially the
way that works is you

00:35:43.417 --> 00:35:45.000
have a certain amount
of time that you

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.070
save by doing that
automation, like maybe you

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:50.610
run this task x times a
day or x times a week.

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:52.830
It takes y amount
of time, and it

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:55.433
would take you z amount
of time to automate it.

00:35:55.433 --> 00:35:56.850
And depending on
those parameters,

00:35:56.850 --> 00:35:59.980
it's either worth it or not
worth it to automate that task.

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:02.725
Now, where the serverless
pattern becomes really useful

00:36:02.725 --> 00:36:05.100
is if you want to make those
sorts of lightweight G Suite

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:07.950
automations-- like I said,
line of business automations--

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:10.440
you can do that,
because serverless

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:11.875
decreases that z for you.

00:36:11.875 --> 00:36:13.500
It decreases the
amount of time that it

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:17.250
would take to set up an
automation for a given task.

00:36:17.250 --> 00:36:19.107
Now, rapid prototyping
is another thing

00:36:19.107 --> 00:36:20.190
that changes a little bit.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:23.760
Notably, rapid prototyping
in a serverless world

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:25.980
means that serverless is
great for gluing together

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:27.570
disparate APIs.

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.270
So let's say you have a
constellation of really

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:32.850
powerful APIs that do
really interesting things.

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:34.565
Then you can quickly
stitch them together

00:36:34.565 --> 00:36:35.940
using things like
Cloud Functions

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:37.980
and our other
serverless offerings

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:41.670
and use that to create easily
scaled apps, which is great

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:44.310
because if you build something
that is the next best thing

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:47.160
but you don't know it yet,
and you tell your friends,

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:49.710
and suddenly you wake
up tomorrow morning

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:53.010
and, like I said, it's on a
national newspaper or something

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:55.020
because it's the best
thing since sliced bread,

00:36:55.020 --> 00:36:58.170
then you want to make sure
you can handle that scaling.

00:36:58.170 --> 00:37:00.300
And that's why serverless
is great for that kind

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:02.420
of workload.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:04.670
Now, a corollary that I
wanted to touch on real

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:08.245
quickly is also how
we do market testing.

00:37:08.245 --> 00:37:09.620
So if you want to
build something

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:12.140
where you're not sure what
the addressable market for it

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:15.680
is serverless is great
because it will give you,

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:17.660
first of all, a
typically smaller upfront

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:20.300
cost than non-serverless
platforms.

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:25.230
Now, this reduced cost
takes place in two ways.

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:29.250
The first is the compute
cost is pay as you go.

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:31.220
So it's very easy
to say, OK, well

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.950
if I don't use as much compute
as I think I'll need to then

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.200
I don't have to commit to a
certain amount of compute,

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:37.520
I can just pay as I go.

00:37:37.520 --> 00:37:40.530
If you underestimate how many
users you're going to get,

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:42.230
then you can say,
OK, well I didn't

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:44.360
have to pay for 10,000
users worth of compute,

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:47.330
I can just pay for,
whatever, 1,000-- however

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:49.130
many users I actually get.

00:37:49.130 --> 00:37:51.030
So you're not on
the hook for that.

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:52.790
Now, if you're in the
opposite situation

00:37:52.790 --> 00:37:55.040
where you think you're going
to get, say, 10,000 users

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:58.670
and you end up getting
100,000, or a million,

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:01.250
the scalability
inherent in serverless

00:38:01.250 --> 00:38:03.560
means that apps can handle
those sudden explosions

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:04.777
in popularity.

00:38:04.777 --> 00:38:06.860
Now of course, you have
to design your application

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:08.270
the right way,
which is why we're

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:09.990
covering design patterns here.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:12.600
But if you do that then
serverless can generally say,

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.500
oh, I understand
you told me that we

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:17.750
were going to get
1,000 users but maybe

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:19.325
we got 10,000 or 100,000.

00:38:19.325 --> 00:38:21.200
No biggie, don't worry
about it, I can scale.

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:28.253
So as far as a quick recap of
everything we covered today,

00:38:28.253 --> 00:38:29.670
the first thing
to bear in mind is

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:32.970
that serverless is more capital
E evolutionary than capital R

00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:34.540
revolutionary.

00:38:34.540 --> 00:38:38.040
So many serverless patterns are
essentially updated versions

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:40.440
of older patterns.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:43.110
And that being said,
serverless does fit neatly

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:45.220
within a lot of these
existing patterns.

00:38:45.220 --> 00:38:46.770
So things like
microservice use can

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:48.990
help us increase our
separation of concerns,

00:38:48.990 --> 00:38:52.050
which can make our code
easier to maintain and reuse.

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:57.230
Also, these service back
ends are a uniquely good fit

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:58.830
for thick client front ends.

00:38:58.830 --> 00:39:01.350
If most of our business
logic is on the front end,

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:02.910
then we can take
advantage of this

00:39:02.910 --> 00:39:05.550
to get a uniquely
lightweight back end.

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:08.348
And of course, how we persist
state has kind of changed.

00:39:08.348 --> 00:39:10.140
When we're dealing with
serverless compute,

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:13.350
we persist that state
outside of compute.

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:15.660
And of course, things like
caching and persisting

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:18.002
your data source may become
increasingly important.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:25.180
Now, one more slide that
I wanted to recap on.

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:27.680
So this asynchronous
mindset is, of course, key

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:29.330
in the serverless environment.

00:39:29.330 --> 00:39:31.520
So we're starting to see
things like decomposition

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:34.310
take hold where we take all
these tasks, we decompose them,

00:39:34.310 --> 00:39:37.370
and then wrap those subtasks
around using various messaging

00:39:37.370 --> 00:39:40.403
strategies such as Pub/Sub,
Cloud Tasks, and HTTP.

00:39:40.403 --> 00:39:42.320
Typically, you'll see
Pub/Sub and Cloud Tasks.

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:45.830
HTTP is less common, but it
does happen in some cases.

00:39:45.830 --> 00:39:47.780
Now of course, the
benefits of this approach.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:50.540
You get, generally speaking,
a better throughput

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:52.680
and scaling performance
in your application.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:55.100
You also get reduced
busy wait cost

00:39:55.100 --> 00:39:57.350
if you're dealing with
long running operations.

00:39:57.350 --> 00:39:59.810
And as I said, not all
of those operations

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:01.700
will conform to that
exact Pub/Sub model.

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:03.950
Sometimes you have to rely
on things like Stackdriver,

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:07.863
or webhooks, or whatever,
but the same logic applies.

00:40:07.863 --> 00:40:09.530
Now, if you're dealing
with flow control

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:12.200
because you have to do some
throttling, or retry logic,

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:13.773
or something like
that, perhaps you

00:40:13.773 --> 00:40:15.440
have some sort of
non-scalable component

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.995
somewhere in your architecture
or some retriable function,

00:40:17.995 --> 00:40:19.370
that's where you're
going to want

00:40:19.370 --> 00:40:20.620
to use something like Pub/Sub.

00:40:20.620 --> 00:40:22.400
And if you need
that compute, you're

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:23.900
going to want to use something
like App Engine or Cloud

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:24.830
Functions.

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:26.900
You may not need that
compute, in which case

00:40:26.900 --> 00:40:28.650
you might just use
Pub/Sub, you might just

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.050
use Cloud Tasks,
something like that.

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:32.630
Now of course,
serverless opens up

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:34.970
a bunch of new
possibilities, like I said.

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:38.780
It's more capital E evolutionary
than capital R revolutionary,

00:40:38.780 --> 00:40:43.040
but it does have its capital
R revolutionary aspects.

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.852
So first of all, how we do
lightweight event processing

00:40:45.852 --> 00:40:47.810
is increasingly being
modernized through things

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:49.940
like functions as a service.

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:51.680
And second, serverless
this enables

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:54.830
us to do rapid prototyping and
rapid scaling at the same time.

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:56.510
They are no longer
mutually exclusive.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:58.670
We don't have to say we
want this to scale rapidly

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:00.560
so we have to set up a
bunch of orchestration

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:02.960
and do a bunch of configuration
to get that scalability.

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:04.972
We can just build our
application more or less,

00:41:04.972 --> 00:41:06.680
throw it on serverless,
and as long as we

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:08.660
design our app in the
right way it will just

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:11.390
automatically scale up and scale
down with whatever workload

00:41:11.390 --> 00:41:13.820
we throw at it.

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:15.620
So that's it for me.

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:17.140
If you have any
questions, please do

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:20.350
reach out to me via email
at faast@google.com.

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:23.620
That's the word fast with two
As, or functions as a service

00:41:23.620 --> 00:41:24.390
my team.

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:26.140
And I couldn't figure
out what the T stood

00:41:26.140 --> 00:41:28.030
for, it just sounded nice.

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:30.670
And then, of course, you can
hit me up on Twitter @serverl0ss

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:31.420
as well.

00:41:31.420 --> 00:41:35.070
